--TEST--
`Core\Zip::extract` charges one bound across the whole archive rather than one per entry, and the breach is a `ParseError` naming the rule — `rule:core-classes/decompression-bound`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `notes/one.txt` holding `first` (5 octets) and `notes/two.txt` holding
// `second` (6), so 11 octets is everything this archive decompresses to.
bytes $ordinary = Core\Encoding::fromBase64("");

// The bound on both sides of the boundary. At 11 the whole archive fits...
echo "at 11 octets: ", Core\Zip::extract($ordinary, "fits", 11), " file(s)\n";

// ...and at 5 the first entry is within the bound on its own, which is exactly
// what a per-entry allowance would have granted the second one as well. It is
// refused because the *archive* has nothing left, which is the same attack one
// level up from a single bomb.
try {
    Core\Zip::extract($ordinary, "spent", 5);
    echo "at 5 octets: wrote them all\n";
} catch (ParseError $bomb) {
    echo $bomb->message, "\n";
}

// A refusal part way leaves what it had already written rather than pretending
// to unwind: the first entry is on the disk and the second never reached it.
echo "spent/notes/one.txt: ", Core\IO::read("spent/notes/one.txt"), "\n";
if (Core\IO::exists("spent/notes/two.txt")) {
    echo "spent/notes/two.txt: written\n";
} else {
    echo "spent/notes/two.txt: never written\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
write = ["."]
--EXPECT--
at 11 octets: 2 file(s)
Core\Zip: an entry decompressing past the 0 octets this archive had left of its 5 octet ceiling is refused rather than truncated (rule:core-classes/decompression-bound): "notes/two.txt". Lower `$maxBytes` or `$maxRatio` to read less; neither can be raised past `[limits] max_decompressed` and `[limits] max_decompression_ratio`.
spent/notes/one.txt: first
spent/notes/two.txt: never written
